        "original_id": "6865edeb-5992-58b0-9423-3347a4ebaf69",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Northwestern University, Middlebury College\n- People: Eddie George\n- Concepts: Hidden Ivies, Little Ivies\n- Places: Evanston, Illinois; Chicago; Doha, Qatar; Washington, D.C.; San Francisco, California; Middlebury, Vermont\n\nFacts:\n- Northwestern University is a private research university based in Evanston, Illinois.\n- Northwestern University has other campuses in Chicago and Doha, Qatar, and it has academic programs and facilities in Washington, D.C., and San Francisco, California.\n- Middlebury College is a private liberal arts college located in Middlebury, Vermont, United States.\n- Post-football, Eddie George earned an MBA from Northwestern University\u0019s Kellogg School of Management.\n- The authors of Hidden Ivies described the Little Ivies as including Amherst, Bowdoin, Middlebury, Swarthmore, Wesleyan, and Williams.\n- The authors of Hidden Ivies said the Little Ivies have reached high prestige and selectivity and reject many highly qualified applicants.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "0251034e-fb29-5e1b-912b-d94bf8169ee2",
        "text": "Page: Northwestern University. Sentence: Northwestern University (NU) is a private research university based in Evanston, Illinois, with other campuses located in Chicago and Doha, Qatar, and academic programs and facilities in Washington, D.C., and San Francisco, California.\nPage: Middlebury College. Sentence: Middlebury College is a private liberal arts college located in Middlebury, Vermont, United States.\nPage: Eddie George. Sentence: Post-football, George earned an MBA from Northwestern University's Kellogg School of Management.\nPage: Hidden Ivies. Sentence: In the introduction, the authors further explain their aim by referring specifically to \"the group historically known as the 'Little Ivies' (including Amherst, Bowdoin, Middlebury, Swarthmore, Wesleyan, and Williams)\" which the authors say have \"scaled the heights of prestige and selectivity and also turn away thousands of our best and brightest young men and women.\"",
